# Quality gate configuration for this repository.
#
# The presence of this file is the opt-in. Modes: off | observe | warn | enforce.
# Every mode below enforce carries a date and a reason — an exception without a
# date is not an exception, it is the new normal.
#
# Assessed 2026-08-20 against the actual tooling in this repo, not a default list.

# --- binding today -----------------------------------------------------------
# All six were verified green before being set to enforce.

build=enforce

# The full suite including the testcontainer integration tests, ~150 s. It was
# unrunnable outside CI until the kafka listener fix, and flaky before the topic
# init fix; three consecutive green runs locally before enforcing.
test=enforce

# gofmt reports no deviation. The line ending noise that used to make a local
# `gofmt -l` flag almost every file is gone: .gitattributes pins eol=lf for this
# repository, so the check means the same thing locally and in CI.
format=enforce

# The criterion is "available fix in a direct dependency". Measured that way the
# repository is green: 5 called findings, all of them transitive and all of them
# unfixed upstream (docker/docker twice, golang-jwt/jwt, jackc/pgx, jackc/
# pgproto3). The one finding that did qualify during the assessment,
# GO-2026-5327 in mongo-driver, is fixed by the version bump that lands with
# this configuration.
vulns=enforce
# Neither the adapter default nor plain govulncheck measures the criterion.
# govulncheck exits non-zero on ANY called vulnerability, including a transitive
# one with no fix available, which the catalog names as the explicit non-goal
# ("transitive findings without a fix — those only make noise"). It would fail
# this gate today with no route to green except weakening it. tools/vulngate
# runs govulncheck, keeps the called findings, and fails only on those that
# have a fix AND sit in a direct requirement or the Go distribution. It prints
# the rest either way, so a green run still names what is known and unfixable.
vulns_cmd="go run ./tools/vulngate"

# Promoted from observe on 2026-08-20, because it is measurably green rather
# than assumed to be: measured on the branch that introduces it, the changed
# lines are covered 40.56 % (831/2049 statements) against a repository average
# of 29.33 % (988/3369). Both figures move with every test that lands, so read
# them as the state at promotion and not as a threshold. The reason the gate was
# only observing — "no coverage report produced yet" — no longer holds now that
# there is a command that produces one.
#
# Note what this does and does not promise. It is the coverage of the *changed*
# lines against the repository average, not an absolute percentage: a repository
# at 29 % can meet it on the first day, and the average only rises from here. It
# is also the one coverage rule that cannot be satisfied by deleting tests.
coverage_delta=enforce
# The adapter has no default for this gate at all — it reports "not implemented"
# and the gate stays a gap. There is also no off-the-shelf command either: the
# criterion needs the diff and the coverage profile read together.
# tools/coveragegate runs the suite with -covermode=set, resolves the base ref
# with the same chain the runner uses, and compares the two ratios by cross
# multiplication rather than as floats, so that "exactly at the average" passes
# instead of depending on the last bit of a division. It needs the full suite,
# so it costs the same ~150 s as the test gate.
coverage_delta_cmd="go run ./tools/coveragegate"

secrets=enforce

# --- not binding yet ---------------------------------------------------------

lint=warn
lint_until=2026-10-01
lint_why="golangci-lint newly introduced on ~6k lines of code that never saw it; baseline has to be measured before new findings can block"

# Enforced since 2026-08-21: the three MPL-2.0 modules that kept this on warn
# (hashicorp errwrap/go-multierror/go-uuid via sarama's kerberos chain) are gone
# — SNRGY-4612 replaced the connector-lib producer with kafka-go, moses bumped
# the library the same day, and go-licenses over the build is green.
deps_license=enforce
# BSD-2-Clause-FreeBSD is on the allowed list here on purpose: it is the BSD
# 2-clause licence with an added disclaimer note, i.e. the permissive licence the
# policy allows under a different spdx id.
deps_license_cmd="go run github.com/google/go-licenses@latest check ./... --allowed_licenses=MIT,Apache-2.0,BSD-2-Clause,BSD-2-Clause-FreeBSD,BSD-3-Clause,ISC,0BSD,Unlicense,CC0-1.0"

# --- not applicable ----------------------------------------------------------

# Go has no separate type check step: the compiler is the type checker and the
# build gate already covers it. Permanent.
typecheck=off

# --- path intensity ----------------------------------------------------------
# Persistence and the places that decide access. A mistake in these either
# corrupts stored data or hands it to the wrong caller, so a pending deadline
# elsewhere must not soften them.
#
# Deliberately NOT all of lib/api: the whole http layer would match almost every
# change and make the escalation meaningless. What matters is where the token is
# read and where ownership is decided — requireUser and mayAccess in
# environment.go, and the owner comparisons in the legacy crud.go.
CRITICAL_PATHS='lib/repo/* lib/state/persistence.go lib/state/crud.go lib/jwt/* lib/api/environment.go'

RELAXED_PATHS='docs/* *.md'

STACK=go
